# One machine, any number of customers' own domains — what Vercel and Render do for you.
#
# Nothing here is magic. A reverse proxy that answers for whatever hostname arrives, gets
# a certificate for it on first request, and hands it to the same app. The app is already
# multi-tenant by Host: `GET /brand?host=…` resolves the white-label brand, so a reseller's
# domain shows their brand without a second deployment.
#
# The one thing that cannot be left out is the `ask` below. A public IP will be sent every
# hostname on earth sooner or later — scanners, stale DNS, people trying it on. A proxy
# that fetched a certificate for each would burn Let's Encrypt's rate limit on our account
# within a day and take every real domain down with it. So the proxy asks the backend
# first, and only a domain a live white-label reseller has actually claimed is allowed.
#
# ── What a reseller does ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   1. Saves their domain in Branding (this is what makes `ask` say yes)
#   2. Points DNS at this machine:
#        A     crm.acme.com  ->  <this server's IPv4>
#      or, if they prefer a CNAME and their DNS host allows it at that level:
#        CNAME crm.acme.com  ->  app.yourplatform.com
#   3. Opens https://crm.acme.com — the certificate is issued on that first request
#
# ── What the platform sets ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   HOSTING_PROVIDER=self        (so the app stops telling people to add the domain
#                                 to Vercel, which does not apply here)
#   CNAME_TARGET=app.yourplatform.com
#   PLATFORM_ROOT_DOMAIN=yourplatform.com
#
# Run:  caddy run --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
# Caddy keeps certificates in its data directory — give it a persistent volume, or it
# re-issues everything on each restart and runs into the rate limit anyway.

{
	email admin@yourplatform.com

	on_demand_tls {
		# Caddy calls this before issuing a certificate for a hostname it has not seen.
		# 200 = go ahead, anything else = refuse. It appends ?domain=<host> itself.
		ask http://127.0.0.1:4000/hosting/domain-allowed

		# A burst of unknown hostnames is what an attack looks like; this caps the damage
		# even if `ask` is somehow wrong.
		interval 2m
		burst 5
	}
}

# ── The platform's own hostnames ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Named explicitly so they never depend on `ask`, and so a backend that is down does not
# take the platform's own login page with it.
yourplatform.com, *.yourplatform.com {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
}

# ── Everyone else's domains ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No hostname list: the empty matcher takes whatever arrives, and `ask` decides whether it
# is served. Same app, same port — the Host header is what makes it theirs.
https:// {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}

	encode zstd gzip

	# The app reads the brand from this, and NextAuth builds callback URLs from it, so a
	# reseller signing in on their own domain stays on their own domain.
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Host {host}
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
	}
}
